{:edb/db-before-t 1633 :edb/db-after-t 1634 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534946 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:45:36.840Z" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119546 1100 "Determine if $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043954/assets/df4f126da2ab3a0661bcb7dc18bb1e5eae8cd48d5c5a1464ea580ccc439195d6.png)" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119546 1100 "Determine if $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534946 false] [17592186119546 1145 "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+8{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0x_1 + 0x_2 = 3,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}.$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119546 1145 "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 8; x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 2; 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} =-5\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} &:=R_{2} - R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} - 2R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} &:=-\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 8R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} & ↔R_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\n0x_1 + 0x_2 = 3\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$." 13194139534946 false] [17592186119547 1100 "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\;\\Pi \\,:\\, \\mathbf{r}=s \\mathbf{b} + t \\mathbf{c}, \\, t,s \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1, 4, 5 \\rangle, \\mathbf{c} = \\langle 2, 4, 6 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{u}= \\langle 2, 10, 12 \\rangle$." 13194139534946 false] [17592186119547 1100 "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\;\\Pi \\,:\\, \\mathbf{r}=s \\mathbf{b} + t \\mathbf{c}, \\, t,s \\in \\mathbb{R},$ where  $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1, 4, 5 \\rangle, \\mathbf{c} = \\langle 2, 4, 6 \\rangle$ and  $\\mathbf{u}= \\langle 2, 10, 12 \\rangle.$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119547 1145 "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsb + tc &= u \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{s + 2t = 2; 4s + 4t = 10; 5s + 6t = 12\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 4R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} - R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{s + 2t = 2; - 4t = 2; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 2t &= 2 \\\\\ns + 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) &= 2 \\\\\ns - 1 &= 2 \\\\\ns &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution\n\n$$\ns=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi$. In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}\n$$" 13194139534946 false] [17592186119547 1145 "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s+2t=2 \\\\ 4s+4t=10 \\\\ 5s+6t=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s+2t=2 \\\\ -4t=2 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s+2t & =2 \\\\ s+2(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ s-1 & =2 \\\\ s & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119595 1100 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043954/assets/966d2d7cd1cd0ef04a1d9b2adc04511dea86cbbf254166988a7072e952879fe0.png)" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119595 1100 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534946 false] [17592186119595 1145 "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=13 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k-91\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{14}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k-91\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-16{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0x_1 + 0x_2 = k-11$. Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0 = k-11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k=11.\n$$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186119595 1145 "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 13; 5x_{1} + 4x_{2} =-5; 7x_{1} + 2x_{2} = k\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 5R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 7R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k - 91\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{14}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k - 91\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 16R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\n0x_1 + 0x_2 = k-11\n$$\n\n. Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0 = k-11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k=11\n$$" 13194139534946 false] [17592186119732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321583 13194139534946 true] [17592186119732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321618 13194139534946 true] [17592186119732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321637 13194139534946 true] [17592186119733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321604 13194139534946 true] [17592186119733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321611 13194139534946 true] [17592186119733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321665 13194139534946 true] [17592186119733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321672 13194139534946 true] [17592186119777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321590 13194139534946 true] [17592186119777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321597 13194139534946 true] [17592186119777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321644 13194139534946 true] [17592186119777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321651 13194139534946 true] [17592186119777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321658 13194139534946 true] [17592186120236 1017 #edb/ref 17592186117916 13194139534946 false] [17592186120236 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321625 13194139534946 true] [17592186120236 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534946 true] [17592186120236 1100 "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b ∈ Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$ from the decomposition $b = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534946 false] [17592186120236 1100 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186120236 1145 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ -7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -7 & -5 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ -7 & -5 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+(-2){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-2)=-6.$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186120239 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534946 true] [17592186120239 1100 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π: r = sb + tc$, $t, s ∈ R$, where $b = ⟨ - 1, 3, 5⟩$, $c = ⟨1,-5,-7⟩$, and $u = ⟨1,-8,-10⟩$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $s \\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u = sb + tc$." 13194139534946 false] [17592186120239 1100 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,3,5⟩$, $c=⟨1,-5,-7⟩$, and  $u=⟨1,-8,-10⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" 13194139534946 true] [17592186120239 1145 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\